<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice (emperor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">murdered <a href="/years/602">602 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maurice was a Byzantine emperor who reigned from 582 until 602. He helped restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> to the Sasanian throne, securing peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. His murder by the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a> in 602 gave Khosrow II a pretext for a long war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> that exhausted both empires and weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-emperor.jpg" alt="Maurice (emperor)" width="330" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Maurice_(transitional_issue).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maurice was born in Arabissus in Cappadocia in 539, the son of a man named Paul, and contemporary sources call him a native Greek speaker. He came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as a notary and served the commander of the imperial bodyguard, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, and when Tiberius was named Caesar in 574 Maurice succeeded him in that command. In late 577, with no military experience, he was made commander-in-chief in the east and fought the war against Sasanian Persia. He failed to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> in 581 and quarrelled with the Ghassanid ruler al-Mundhir III, whom he accused of treason, a charge modern historians dismiss.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The dying Tiberius II named Maurice his heir and son-in-law, and Maurice was crowned on 13 August 582. He inherited a bankrupt empire at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, paying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> 80,000 gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a> a year, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. In 590 the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormizd-iv">Hormizd IV</a> was overthrown and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Although the Senate advised against it, Maurice helped him regain his throne, and in 591 the combined forces defeated the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a>. Khosrow ceded western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and the empire no longer paid tribute to Persia.</p><p>From 591 Maurice turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, retaking Singidunum in 592 and crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the first Roman emperor to do so in over two centuries. In the west he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Italy</a> in 584, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, and the Exarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 591. His fiscal measures were unpopular: in 588 he cut army pay by a quarter, and the troops on the Persian front mutinied, and about 599 he refused to pay a small ransom for 12,000 soldiers held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, who killed them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In autumn 602 Maurice ordered the army to winter beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and the exhausted troops mutinied and proclaimed an officer named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a>. Maurice left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by ship on 22 November, Phocas was crowned on 23 November, and Maurice was captured and murdered at Chalcedon on 27 November 602 after watching five of his sons executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> used the murder as a pretext for a war of twenty-six years that devastated both empires before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a>. The Strategikon, a manual of war, is traditionally attributed to Maurice, though some historians now think it the work of his brother or another general.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice (emperor)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Arabissus, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Harbor of Eutropius. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.24,36.91&amp;year=602" aria-label="Arabissus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5468 790 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maurice (emperor)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5918 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5918 1060h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.24,36.91&amp;year=602">What was here in 602 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
